<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Špilberk Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Spielberg Castle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Špilberk Castle stands on a hill above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. Founded in the thirteenth century as a royal castle, it withstood a Swedish siege in 1645, was rebuilt as a baroque fortress and from the eighteenth century served as a notorious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> prison for political prisoners, including Italian patriots such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silvio-pellico">Silvio Pellico</a>. It is now the museum of the city of Brno.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spilberk-castle.jpg" alt="Špilberk Castle" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kirk · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%A0pilberk_(02).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Špilberk Castle stands on a hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. Its construction began as early as the first half of the 13th century under the Přemyslid kings and was completed by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a>. From a major royal castle established about the middle of that century, and the seat of the Moravian margraves in the middle of the 14th century, it was gradually turned into a huge baroque citadel, which was considered the harshest prison in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> and was afterwards converted into barracks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> bought the castle in 1560 and made it a municipal fortress. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> on 8 November 1620 the leading Moravian members of the anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> insurrection were imprisoned there for several years. Its bastion fortifications helped Brno withstand Swedish raids in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and the successful defence led to further fortification.</p><p>The castle served as a prison alongside its military role. Protestants were the first prisoners, followed by participants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a>–49. Franz Freiherr von der Trenck was jailed there and died on 4 October 1749. Jean-Baptiste Drouet, who had arrested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>, and fifteen Hungarian Jacobins led by Ferenc Kazinczy were held there. From 1822 specially built cells for state prisoners in the northern wing held Italian Carbonari, among them the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silvio-pellico">Silvio Pellico</a>, who served eight years and made the prison known across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with his book My Prisons. The last large group of political prisoners was nearly 200 Polish revolutionaries, mostly from the Kraków Uprising of 1846. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> dissolved the prison in 1855.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the last prisoners left three years later, the buildings became barracks for the next hundred years. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> military prisoners and civilian objectors to the Austro-Hungarian regime were held there, and in the first year of the Nazi occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> several thousand Czech patriots suffered there, some being put to death, while for most it was a station on the way to German prisons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>. The Czechoslovak army left in 1959, and the following year the castle became the seat of the Brno City Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Špilberk Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As a land of the Bohemian crown, Moravia had no standing army of its own; its defence rested on noble levies summoned by the margrave or the diet, on royal castles such as <b>Špilberk</b> above Brno, and on walled towns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Lombardy-Venetia had an efficient administration, good schools and a prosperous economy, but censorship and police repression made Austria unpopular; the patriot Silvio Pellico described his imprisonment in the <b>Spielberg</b> fortress.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lombardy-venetia">Kingdom of Lombardy-Venetia</a></li></ul></section>
